= Introduction

To measure a part, you usually touch it: calipers, a coordinate-measuring machine, a gauge pin. A photograph touches nothing, yet it already contains the part's dimensions, mixed up with its pose, its finish, the light in the room and the lens. Separating them is inverse rendering: find the parameters whose rendering matches the photograph. For an engineered object those parameters are not free-form vertices or a neural field. They are the dimensions in a CAD model, the angles of a mechanism's joints and the finish of its surfaces. If a renderer can recover them, and say how well it knows each one, it is a measuring instrument.

An instrument has two obligations. It must be *exact*: the same input gives the same reading, and the reading means what it says. And it must be *precise*: its error is small and known. For a differentiable renderer, exactness is a property of the gradient. Path replay backpropagation @vicini2021prb made physically based differentiable rendering practical by replacing the stored path tape with a second, replayed pass. That only works if the replay regenerates exactly the paths the forward pass drew; otherwise the gradient belongs to a different estimator from the one rendered. Systems built on general array compilers @jakob2022drjit meet this contract inside one process and one backend. We wanted a GPU tier and an f64 CPU reference that run the same material source and agree to rounding, with each tier bit-identical across runs. Then a failing gradient test is a bug, never noise, and a reading can be trusted.

This paper describes irys, the renderer behind vcad and our robotics tools, and how it meets both obligations. Its contributions are:

- *One BSDF source, two lowerings, two tiers.* Materials are tang tensor graphs. tang lowers each one to an evaluation program and an adjoint program, emitting WGSL kernels for the GPU and running the same graph in its f64 interpreter for the CPU reference. The path logic (ray generation, traversal, bounce, accumulation) is hand-written (@sec:ir).
- *PRB on a deterministic wavefront with hero wavelengths and NEE.* The estimator is Vicini et al.'s with next-event estimation, as in Mitsuba 3; what is new is a wavefront whose replay is bit-identical, with four wavelengths per path and the light-sampled lanes folded into the same graph batch (@sec:prb).
- *Bit-exact replay by construction.* An integer RNG that is exact on both tiers, fixed-order reductions with no float atomics, and a BVH walk that depends only on the ray (@sec:det).
- *Pose gradients through instanced CAD meshes.* Primary edge sampling with crease ownership and visibility-aware importance, an interior term by forward-mode duals with attached frames, and secondary edge sampling for moving shadows and reflections (@sec:pose).
- *Validation at every layer and a fit on a real robot.* Gradients against central differences with common random numbers, GPU against CPU f64, and a joint pose and finish recovery on an arm read from its CAD source (@sec:results).
- *Dimensions from photographs.* A search over editable CAD programs, scored by description length and fitted with the same edge-sampling estimator, recovers the dimensions of parts from synthetic phone photographs beside a printed tag (@sec:recon).
- *A bridge to CAD parameters.* A per-vertex image gradient that the vcad BRep kernel pulls back to the dimensions of the part, shared as a benchmark with the companion paper @pedersen2026vcad (@sec:brep).

== Scope and limits

- *Determinism is per device and driver.* The GPU tier runs in f32, so its results agree with the f64 reference to rounding, not bitwise; it is bit-identical against itself on one device and driver.
- *Materials.* The differentiable material is a GGX conductor with a three-band spectral Fresnel. Diffuse surfaces and the environment are not differentiated. Gaussian splats are rendered but not differentiated.
- *Lights.* The environment is the only emitter (a sky and a von Mises–Fisher sun lobe).
- *Pose parameters are rigid.* Joint angles and the camera move instances rigidly. Non-rigid shape change enters only through the BRep bridge of @sec:brep.
- *The pose fit has a basin.* The elbow converges from within about ±#V("basin.deg")° of the answer and fails from outside it; the blur and pyramid schedules we tried did not widen it reliably.
- *Photographs are synthetic.* Every photograph in @sec:recon is rendered, with a camera model applied. Real photographs of printed parts, checked with calipers, are the next experiment.
- *Hardware.* All timings come from one Apple M-series machine through Metal; the exact chip was not recorded in the source logs, and timings in this draft are not yet regenerated on a single pinned machine (@sec:repro).

= Background and related work

*Differentiable path tracing.* Li et al. introduced edge sampling to account for visibility discontinuities in Monte Carlo rendering @li2018edge. Radiative backpropagation @nimierdavid2020rb and path replay backpropagation @vicini2021prb reduced the memory of the adjoint pass to a constant per path; Dr.Jit provides a tracing compiler for such methods @jakob2022drjit. Visibility can also be handled by reparameterisation @loubet2019reparam, by warped-area sampling @bangaru2020warped, or by projective sampling @zhang2023projective; we compared warped estimators for the secondary term and chose edge sampling (@sec:pose).

*Spectral rendering.* Hero wavelength sampling @wilkie2014hero traces several wavelengths along one path, the hero choosing directions. We use it unchanged and make each wavelength its own PRB lane.

*Compilers for rendering.* SLANG.D adds automatic differentiation to a shading language @bangaru2023slangd and is the closest analogue to writing BSDFs as tang graphs. Laine et al. argue for splitting a path tracer into wavefront kernels @laine2013megakernels, the structure we use.

*Differentiable rendering systems.* Mitsuba 3 @jakob2022mitsuba3 implements path replay with next-event estimation and multiple importance sampling, projective sampling and reparameterisation on Dr.Jit, and is the closest system to irys's differentiable core. irys differs in what it guarantees rather than in the estimator: a replay that is bit-identical to the forward pass by construction, an f64 reference that runs the same material source, spectral transport with hero wavelengths, and visibility terms adapted to CAD tessellations (coplanar slivers, crease ownership, attached frames).

*Inverse CAD and program synthesis.* Recovering an editable CAD description from observations is usually posed as program synthesis. CSGNet parses shapes into CSG programs with a recurrent network @sharma2018csgnet; InverseCSG converts meshes to compact CSG trees with a solver-based synthesizer @du2018inversecsg; Szalinski rewrites flat CSG into programs with loops by equality saturation @nandi2020szalinski; DeepCAD generates sketch-and-extrude sequences @wu2021deepcad; ShapeAssembly writes part-assembly programs @jones2020shapeassembly; and Point2CAD recovers BRep surfaces from point clouds @liu2024point2cad. Ellis et al. infer graphics programs from images by proposing primitives and synthesizing a program over them @ellis2018graphics, and DreamCoder learns a library of program components alongside the search @ellis2021dreamcoder. These methods take meshes, point clouds or clean drawings as input, or learn a prior from a dataset; ours starts from photographs and scores each candidate program by rendering it, with gradients through the renderer for its dimensions and a description length that weighs residual, parameter cost and program length in one unit. CAD parameters upstream of the mesh are differentiated by our companion paper's BRep kernel @pedersen2026vcad, joined to irys in @sec:brep.

*Measurement from images.* Close-range photogrammetry measures manufactured objects from calibrated images, usually by triangulating targets or features and adjusting the cameras and points together @luhmann2019photogrammetry. Our recovery shares its tools (a calibration target, bundle-adjustment-style least squares, Fisher-information error estimates) but fits a parametric model of the whole part to its pixels rather than to sparse points, so a hole's diameter is read from its outline and shading rather than from markers.

= Materials as compiled graphs <sec:ir>



The differentiable material has parameters $theta = (alpha, F_0^(450), F_0^(550), F_0^(650))$: GGX roughness with height-correlated Smith masking, and a Schlick Fresnel whose $F_0$ is interpolated between three spectral hats. It is written once as a tang `TensorGraph` whose inputs are per-lane columns (the cosines at a vertex and the hero wavelength's hat weights) and whose $theta$ is broadcast. tang lowers it two ways. The *eval* program returns $f$ and its pdf. The *adjoint* program is the backward pass of $sum_j delta f_j dot f_j$, so the batch sum over lanes is the reduction to $delta theta$. Each program is emitted as WGSL, one kernel per materialised comprehension, or as compiled C in f32 and an interpreter in f64 for the CPU. The interpreter matches a hand-written f64 GGX to $10^(-12)$, and the compiled C matches the interpreter to #E("p1.c_vs_interp") in the worst relative error.

The path logic is hand-written, in WGSL for the GPU and in Rust for the CPU reference: ray generation, BVH traversal, bounce, the throughput update, the final splat and accumulation, and the device-side loss scoring. tang's comprehensions are straight-line bodies over fixed shapes, so data-dependent path loops and a traversal stack cannot be expressed in them today. Sampling the GGX visible normals is detached, since it needs no derivative under PRB, and the divisor pdf is the sampler's, which we test against the graph's pdf to $10^(-9)$.

= Path replay on a wavefront <sec:prb>

*Estimator.* For a path with vertices $j$ and radiance $L$, BSDF-only PRB accumulates $delta L (L - L_j) partial_theta log f_j$ at each vertex, where $L_j$ is the radiance collected before vertex $j$. With next-event estimation and multiple importance sampling the gradient becomes

$ partial L \/ partial theta = sum_j [ partial_theta log f_j (omega_(B,j)) (L - L_j^+) + partial_theta log f_j (omega_(L,j)) C_j ] $

where $omega_B$ is the BSDF-sampled direction, $omega_L$ the light-sampled one, $C_j$ the NEE contribution at $j$ and $L_j^+$ the radiance up to and including it. The MIS weights are functions of detached pdfs and therefore constants under $partial_theta$. In the wavefront the light-sampled lanes are the second half of one graph batch, so a single adjoint dispatch serves both strategies. A nonlinear loss needs the primal image before the adjoint, so the loss is scored between the forward and replay passes; seeding the adjoint from the same pass instead biased roughness from 0.6 to 1.0 in our first spike.

*Hero wavelengths.* Each path carries four stratified wavelengths, the hero and its rotations by $k \/ 4$ of the visible range @wilkie2014hero. Only the hero draws directions. The wavefront's lanes are (path, wavelength) pairs, four times as many as paths, and each lane is its own PRB path. At equal paths, four wavelengths cut per-pixel variance #F("hero.var_total", 1)× in total and #F("hero.var_chroma", 1)× in chroma.

*Memory.* PRB stores nothing per vertex. With four wavelengths a path costs #V("hero.words_4l") f64 words at depth 2 and at depth 8 alike. Memory scales with paths, not pixels. Frames larger than one dispatch are traced in chunks, and a #(1024)² image at 4 spp, #V("mega.lanes") lanes, replays bit-identically in nine chunks.

#figure(fig-replay(),
  caption: [#lead[Path replay, drawn.] The forward pass computes a path's radiance and keeps a fixed handful of words per path, nothing per vertex. The replay regenerates the same path from the same seed through the same pipelines, bit for bit, and accumulates $delta theta$ at each vertex. Memory stays constant in depth, where an AD tape would grow with it.],
) <fig:replay>

= Determinism <sec:det>

PRB's replay must redraw the forward pass's paths. irys makes this hold in four ways.

- *Same pipelines.* Forward and replay dispatch the same compute pipelines over the same bind groups; they differ only in a uniform that enables the store of $delta f$.
- *An integer RNG on both tiers.* SplitMix64 is ported onto pairs of 32-bit words built from 16-bit limbs, so the GPU, which lacks 64-bit integers, produces exactly the CPU's stream. A uniform keeps the top 24 of 53 bits, so both tiers walk the same paths up to f32 rounding of the geometry.
- *No float atomics.* The reduction over lanes is tang's monoid reduction, split 256 ways with one workgroup per parameter and a fixed combining tree. Splat accumulation runs in bounce order, loss scoring uses a fixed stride and tree, chunks are summed in order, and the device prefix sum used by edge sampling has a fixed order.
- *A walk that depends only on the ray.* BVH traversal uses a fixed-size stack, visits the near child first by slab entry with a deterministic tie-break, and culls by the closest hit so far. The walk, and therefore the floating-point sequence, is a function of the ray alone, not of thread scheduling.

The result is that the replay reproduces the forward radiance bit for bit on every lane, #V("gpu.replay_lanes") of #V("gpu.replay_lanes") at depths 2 and 8, with NEE, and through the two-level instanced scenes and a garden scene of meshes and Gaussian splats; two runs give bitwise-identical loss and $delta theta$. The guarantee holds per device and driver. A different GPU or compiler may round differently, and then agrees with the CPU reference only to f32 precision.

#figure(fig-determinism(),
  caption: [#lead[Three choices make the replay bit-exact.] (a) SplitMix64 on 16-bit limbs gives the GPU, which has no 64-bit integers, the CPU's exact stream. (b) Lane gradients are summed by a fixed tree, never by float atomics. (c) BVH traversal visits children in an order that depends only on the ray.],
) <fig:det>

= Pose gradients through instanced CAD <sec:pose>

*Scenes.* A scene is a two-level BVH: one bottom-level tree per CAD part or group, and a top-level tree over instances, each with an object-to-world transform. irys maps rays into an instance's space without renormalising them, so hit distances compare across instances. A pose $phi$, such as joint angles, is a map from parameters to the transforms of the moving instances. The full robot in this paper has 2.4 million triangles, tessellated by vcad from its CAD source (a text format of parametric solids).

*Primary edges.* Moving an instance moves the curves across which the radiance arriving at the camera plane jumps: silhouettes, boundaries and creases between faces that are not coplanar. By Reynolds transport, a pixel's derivative includes

#figure(fig-edge(),
  caption: [#lead[Primary edge sampling.] Moving an edge under $phi$ changes which pixels it covers, a jump that no derivative of shading at fixed rays can see. Paired rays traced either side of a sampled point measure it. On CAD tessellations the edge's own triangle is often a sliver thinner than $epsilon$, so the inner ray counts as inside on any coplanar triangle of the same face. The inset is not to scale.],
) <fig:edge>

$ partial I_p \/ partial phi supset.eq integral_(E inter p) (f(s - epsilon n) - f(s + epsilon n)) v_n (s) dif ell (s) $

over the projected edges $E$, where $n$ is the edge's outward normal and $v_n$ the normal speed of the material point under $phi$ @li2018edge. We sample edges with density proportional to screen length times an importance (1 for silhouettes, a dihedral weight for creases, a small weight for back-facing edges), trace the two sides $10^(-3)$ pixels apart with common random numbers, and require the inner ray to hit the edge's own triangle or a coplanar neighbour. CAD tessellations are full of slivers thinner than $epsilon$, and without that coplanar rule the estimator loses most silhouette samples. A crease between two front faces is owned by the face that meets the eye more squarely, so its jump is counted exactly once. A low-resolution buffer of which instance each pixel sees lets the sampler demote edges it shows hidden, which cut variance #F("idbuf.var", 1)× at equal samples. Sampling runs on the device, with a fixed-order prefix sum; it took the edge term on the arm from #V("edge.total_before_ms") ms to #V("edge.total_after_ms") ms per iteration.

*Interior term and attached frames.* Away from discontinuities the estimator is smooth in $phi$ at fixed random numbers, and we differentiate it by forward-mode dual numbers through a re-trace of the frame: ray–triangle intersection with the corners as duals, the shading frame, GGX sampling and evaluation, NEE and the MIS weights. The renderer builds a vertex's tangent frame from its normal with a construction that flips sign when the normal crosses a plane, a discontinuity in $phi$ at fixed random numbers that no edge term sees. We instead differentiate the estimator whose frame on a moving instance is rigidly attached, $R(phi) R(phi_0)^T "basis"(n(phi_0))$. At $phi_0$ it is the rendered estimator exactly, and for every $phi$ it is an equally valid estimator of the same image, so its derivative is an unbiased estimate of $partial I \/ partial phi$ from the paths the forward pass already drew. The duals reproduce the forward pass's values to #E("int.dual_vs_fwd").

#figure(fig-frames(),
  caption: [#lead[Attached frames.] Building the tangent frame from the normal flips its sign when the normal crosses a plane, so a direction drawn at fixed random numbers jumps, and no edge term sees it (a). A frame rigidly carried by the instance turns with it (b). Both are valid estimators of the same image; only the attached one is differentiable at fixed random numbers. Drawn in 2D, where the flip is a reversal.],
) <fig:frames>

*Secondary edges.* When an instance moves, its shadow and its reflection move too. We sample the edges seen from each path vertex with a per-tree edge BVH, under one-sample multiple importance over three densities: geometric, toward the sun's cone, and toward the mirror lobe. The boundary term includes the velocity of the BSDF-sampled direction, obtained by inverting the sampler at the path's random numbers and redrawing it in duals. Against warped-area estimators on the same configurations, edge sampling had 14–19× lower variance at 1.6× lower cost.

#figure(fig-secondary(),
  caption: [#lead[Moving shadows.] An occluder's light-silhouette edge casts a shadow edge on its receiver; when the occluder moves by $v$, the shadow edge moves by $d S = v - l (m dot v) \/ (m dot l)$, and every pixel it crosses changes, whether or not the occluder is in view.],
) <fig:secondary>

*Camera.* The same machinery differentiates a seven-parameter camera (translation, rotation, field of view), with the eye, axes and field of view as duals and every instance's edges as candidates.

= Results <sec:results>

Unless stated, we check gradients against central differences (CD) of the same estimator with common random numbers; "frozen" means the random numbers are fixed so the CD is noise-free. Monte Carlo comparisons report a z-score, the difference over its standard error. Timings are release builds on the M-series machine above. The number in each table is read from `data/measured.tsv`, whose rows give the source test and commit.

